# not_projection — THE CLAIM, checked against a DERIVED domain.
#
# `projection_kind` is NOT listed anywhere. It is every expr::Code in
# include/logos/compiler/lir_schema.hpp MINUS this file. That inversion is the
# whole point and it is deliberate:
#
#   listing the PROJECTIONS  -> a new code is silently not one   (blind, quiet)
#   listing the EXEMPTIONS   -> a new code IS one until claimed   (loud)
#
# The first shape is the defect these rules exist to find; the detector must not
# be built in it. A new expr code now flags every walker until someone writes a
# line here and says why. That noise is the feature.
#
# A PROJECTION is a step from a place to a sub-place. Everything below is
# exempt because it PRODUCES A VALUE rather than naming a sub-place of one.

# literals — construct a value from nothing
LitInt
LitFloat
LitBool
LitStr
EnumLit
EnumLitData
StructLit
ArrLit
TupleLit
SliceLit
WritLit

# calls — the result is a new value, and the callee's places are not ours
Call
MethodCall
ClosureBox
ClosureCall
FnPtrCall
FormatCall

# operators — compute over values
BinOp
Unary
Cast
PtrArith
PtrDiff

# reference FORMATION: `&x` is where a walk ENDS, not a step it takes.
# The walker's caller decomposes the operand; the AddrOf node itself is a value.
AddrOf
AddrOfTemp

# control flow yielding a value — a place cannot be projected through a branch
IfExpr
MatchExpr
Try
BlockExpr

# compile-time / reflection — no runtime place at all
SizeOf
AlignOf
TypeCodeOf
ReflectOf
GenericRef
PackExpand

# slice DECOMPOSITION accessors: these read a slice's fat-pointer halves and
# yield a scalar (len, data ptr). `SliceIndex` is the projection; these are not.
SliceLen
SlicePtr

# VarRef is the place ROOT, not a step: it is where a walk TERMINATES, and it
# is claimed as such in place_root_kind. Exempt here so it is not demanded of
# walkers as a projection they failed to handle.
VarRef
